        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: പതിനൊന്നാം",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "പതിനൊന്നാം (pathinonnāṁ) — \"eleventh\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "പതിനൊന്ന് was itself a built word: pathin-, the joining shape of ten, with onnŭ welded on. Now the ordinal ending goes on top of that, and the result is a word of three parts, not one of which had to be learned as a whole."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "പതിനൊന്നാം ദിവസം (pathinonnāṁ divasam), the eleventh day."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: the ending is a fact about numbers, not about ten",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Most books stop an ordinal chapter at tenth, and a learner reasonably concludes that those ten words were the set. In Malayalam there is no set. ആം attaches to a number, so every number you learn from here on arrives with its ordinal already made: ഇരുപത് gives ഇരുപതാം (irupathāṁ), twentieth, without a lesson."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That is why this book taught the ending in the very first lesson of the previous chapter rather than after ten examples. The examples were never the content."
            }
          ]
        },
